    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>

<p>Consider the function \(f(x)=\tan(2x)\).</p>

<p>The standard tangent function \(y=\tan x\) has period \(\pi\). The factor \(2\) multiplying \(x\) changes the period and the locations of the zeros and vertical asymptotes.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1: Period</strong></p>

<p>For a function of the form \(f(x)=\tan(bx)\), the period is \(\displaystyle \frac{\pi}{|b|}\).</p>

<p>Here, \(b=2\), so the period is
\(\displaystyle T=\frac{\pi}{2}\).</p>


<p>The correct answer is \(\displaystyle \frac{\pi}{2}\).</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Zeros</strong></p>

<p>The tangent function is zero when its argument is an integer multiple of \(\pi\). Therefore,</p>

<p>\(2x=n\pi\), where \(n\in\mathbb{Z}\).</p>

<p>Solving for \(x\) gives \(\displaystyle x=\frac{n\pi}{2}\).</p>

<p>In the interval \(\displaystyle -\frac{\pi}{2}\leq x\leq\frac{\pi}{2}\), the zeros are</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle x=-\frac{\pi}{2},\quad x=0,\quad x=\frac{\pi}{2}\).</p>

<p>Your first zero: \(\displaystyle {@ta_zero1@}\).</p>
<p>Your second zero: \(\displaystyle {@ta_zero2@}\).</p>
<p>Your third zero: \(\displaystyle {@ta_zero3@}\).</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Vertical asymptotes</strong></p>

<p>Vertical asymptotes occur when the argument of the tangent function equals \(\displaystyle \frac{\pi}{2}+n\pi\).</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>\(2x=\frac{\pi}{2}+n\pi\).</p>

<p>Dividing by \(2\) gives</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle x=\frac{\pi}{4}+\frac{n\pi}{2}\).</p>

<p>The two vertical asymptotes nearest the origin are
\(\displaystyle x=-\frac{\pi}{4}\) and
\(\displaystyle x=\frac{\pi}{4}\).</p>

<p>Your left asymptote: \(\displaystyle {@ta_asymL@}\).</p>
<p>Your right asymptote: \(\displaystyle {@ta_asymR@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
